                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The game was released as an expanded version of “Torn Tales: Rebound,” but for some reason, even though I've purchased it, I can only play one mission before the game informs me that it's a demo... “Torn Tales: Rebound” differs not only in its higher difficulty level right from the start, but also features co-op, more loot, and crafting. Therefore, this review focuses on the original game, and despite mixed reviews, I personally view it positively.  The developer has included three interesting characters—Dr. Jekyll & Mr. Hyde, Snow White, and Robin Hood—but their personalities are by no means fairy-tale-like. The gameplay is enjoyable once the characters are maxed out and fully equipped; however, bosses and larger groups of enemies will pose a greater challenge at the start—after completing the standard difficulty level, you can try your hand at replaying the game on Hardcore mode. You also gain access to the Arena, where you face waves of enemies—the fun continues until either you’re defeated or the statue is destroyed. Probably the biggest drawbacks of Torn Tales include the sparsely populated maps (some with many branching paths), and the scattered groups of enemies aren’t enough to fill these maps. The three characters move sluggishly, sometimes lose their way, and don’t attack, making it easy to lose a team member in a tougher battle. The graphics are very basic; they resemble games from around 2004–2005. I’d love to see a remake of Torn Tales, because this indie title offers an interesting experience that lasts a few hours, so the game doesn’t get tedious due to its length. That said, it would be necessary to make appropriate balance adjustments, upgrade the graphics to modern standards, and fill the empty maps with appropriate content - enemies, dungeons, and treasures.\n",
